# T33413 -- (280) Management_Trap - Temperature -- run 2 working log
# tb277, DUT swi_d (AT-x960-56YSQ), console /dev/u2. Group snmpmon-2026-10-20T0825.
# Written as the run went. Run is CLEAN.

## Why there is a run 2

Run 1 (`work/run1.log`, graded PARTIAL) concluded that the over-temperature trap was
**NOT MEASURABLE on this bench**. That conclusion was WRONG, and run 2 exists to replace it.

Run 1 reasoned entirely from the **CLI surface**: `temperature ?` and `environment ?` are
`% Unrecognized command` in config mode, `snmp-server enable trap ?` offers no `temperature`
group, and every limit in `show system environment` is reported by the hardware rather than
configured. All of that is true, and all of it is beside the point, because **the threshold is
writable underneath the CLI.** An AlliedWare Plus switch is Linux, and the sensor limits live in
the kernel's hwmon tree in `/sys`, reachable from the CLI's own `start-shell`.

The Test Engineer supplied the method, demonstrated by him on an AT-x250-28XTm:

    AT-x250-28XTm# start-shell
    [root@AT-x250-28XTm flash]# echo 20000 > /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon0/temp1_max
    [root@AT-x250-28XTm hwmon0]# exit
    AT-x250-28XTm# 20:45:47 AT-x250-28XTm Platform: SENSOR Board Base - Temp: Remote 1:
                  Alarm asserted. Reading:37 Threshold:20 (Degrees C)

🔴 **READ THIS BEFORE READING ANY RESULT BELOW. THE SWITCH DID NOT GET HOT.**
Everything in this run was produced by **lowering the alarm threshold below a normal room
temperature reading**. The sensor reading is real, live and entirely normal throughout --
**27 degrees C, from first read to last**. The only thing that moved is the LIMIT the platform
compares that reading against, from its hardware default of 61 C down to 20 C and back to 61 C.
No heat was applied to the unit, no fan was interfered with, and at no point was the unit in
any thermal danger. Any sentence below that says "the alarm asserted" or "fault" means
"27 is greater than the 20 I told it to use", nothing more.

**Authority.** `start-shell` is root **on the device under test**, which STANDING-ORDERS §1
puts explicitly in play ("You may reconfigure and deconfigure devices as required to perform a
test"). STANDING-ORDERS §4 reserves root on **the testbox**, which is a different machine and
was never touched. No permission was needed for this and none was waited for.

**Run 1 is not deleted and is not edited.** Each run stands alone; `/create-logs` uses the
latest. Where run 2 reuses a fact run 1 measured, it says so rather than re-measuring it.

