## STEP 2 -- finding the sensor underneath: the hwmon tree, and which node is Front Right

The x960-56YSQ has fourteen temperature sensors where the Test Engineer's x250 example had few,
so `hwmon0/temp1_max` is NOT the right node here and had to be found rather than assumed.

Driven with **`tools/awshell.py`** (new in this run; see "Tools" below), through
`tools/tb awshell tb277 cksnmpmon u2 115200 --timeout 60 '<shell command>' ...`.
File `work/r2-s1-hwmon-enum.log`, 15:47:33 NZDT:

    >>> uname -a
    Linux awplus 6.12.110 #1 SMP PREEMPT Tue Oct  6 01:02:01 UTC 2026 aarch64 GNU/Linux

    >>> for h in /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon*; do echo "=== $h name=$(cat $h/name) dev=..."; done
    === /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon0 name=f212a200mdiomii00  (platform/.../f212a200.mdio-mii:00)
    === /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon1 name=adt7476            (i2c-1 address 0x2d)
    === /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon2 name=adt7476            (i2c-1 address 0x2e)
    === /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon3 name=max31343           (i2c-1 address 0x68)
    === /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon4 name=ps_2162_6l2        (i2c-7 address 0x58)
    === /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon5 name=ps_2162_6l2        (i2c-8 address 0x58)

Every `temp*` attribute of all six was then read (`work/r2-s2-hwmon-temps.log`, 15:48 NZDT) and
matched against the STEP 1 CLI table by **reading, low limit and high limit together** -- three
values agreeing at once, not just one. The mapping is exact (hwmon reports milli-degrees):

| CLI sensor (resource 100) | CLI reading / low / high | hwmon node | `tempN_input` | `tempN_min` | `tempN_max` |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **6 Temp: Front Right** | **27 / -11 / 61** | **hwmon1 (adt7476 @0x2d) temp1** | **27250** | **-11000** | **61000** |
| 7 Temp: Rear Right | 33 / -11 / 70 | hwmon1 temp2 | 32750 | -11000 | 70000 |
| 8 Temp: Front Switch | 29 / -11 / 77 | hwmon1 temp3 | 29250 | -11000 | 77000 |
| 16 Temp: Front Left | 31 / -11 / 61 | hwmon2 (adt7476 @0x2e) temp1 | 30500 | -11000 | 61000 |
| 17 Temp: Rear Left | 34 / -11 / 67 | hwmon2 temp2 | 34000 | -11000 | 67000 |
| 18 Temp: Rear Switch | 34 / -11 / 77 | hwmon2 temp3 | 33500 | -11000 | 77000 |
| 101/8 PSU 1 Ambient | 37 / - / 65 | hwmon4 temp1 | 37250 | - | max 65000 |
| 101/9 PSU 1 Second | 72 / - / 105 | hwmon4 temp2 | 72250 | - | **rated_max** 105000 |
| 101/10 PSU 1 Primary | 53 / - / 95 | hwmon4 temp3 | 52750 | - | **rated_max** 95000 |
| 102/8,9,10 PSU 2 | 40 / 51 / 46 | hwmon5 temp1,2,3 | 39750 / 51500 / 45750 | - | as above |

Three things worth writing down for whoever does this next:

- **The two board-sensor groups are two separate adt7476 chips**, 0x2d and 0x2e on i2c-1.
  CLI sensors 6/7/8 are hwmon1's temp1/2/3; CLI sensors 16/17/18 are hwmon2's. The CLI numbering
  is not the hwmon numbering, and there is no label file -- the join has to be made on the
  values.
- **`hwmon0` is NOT a board temperature.** It is the MDIO PHY (`temp1_input 21000`,
  `temp1_crit 100000`, no `temp1_max`) and it appears nowhere in `show system environment`.
  Writing to it would have proved nothing. The Test Engineer's x250 example used `hwmon0`
  because on that product `hwmon0` IS the board sensor; **on this product it is not**, which is
  exactly why the dispatch said to enumerate first.
- **The two SoC sensors (CLI 21 SoC AP and 22 SoC SB) have no hwmon node at all.** They are not
  in `/sys/class/hwmon` and so could not have been used for this case.
- For the PSU sensors the CLI's high limit comes from **`tempN_rated_max`**, not `tempN_max`
  (hwmon4/5 report `temp2_max 65000` while the CLI shows 105). A test that wrote `temp2_max`
  on a PSU would therefore not be writing the limit the CLI is showing. **This is why the
  chassis sensor, where `tempN_max` and the CLI high limit are the same number, was chosen.**

**STEP 2 PASS** -- the node that backs `Temp: Front Right` is
**`/sys/class/hwmon/hwmon1/temp1_max`**, identified by agreement on three independent values.

