## STEP 4 -- lower the threshold below the live reading; the alarm asserts

🔴 **Before anything was written, the original value was read and recorded byte for byte.**
A sysfs write is NOT in `show running-config`, so `rcdiff.py` cannot see it and cannot prove it
was put back. The exact bytes are the only restore proof there is.

`work/r2-s5-lower-threshold.log`, box 15:51:15 NZDT:

    >>> ls -l /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon1/temp1_max
    -rw-r--r--    1 root     root          4096 Oct 16 06:22 /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon1/temp1_max

    >>> cat /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon1/temp1_max
    61000
    >>> od -c /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon1/temp1_max
    0000000    6   1   0   0   0  \n
    0000006
    >>> wc -c /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon1/temp1_max
    6 /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon1/temp1_max
    >>> cat /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon1/temp1_input
    27000
    >>> cat /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon1/temp1_alarm
    0

**ORIGINAL VALUE: the six bytes `61000\n`.** Then the one change this case makes:

    >>> echo 20000 > /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon1/temp1_max      (DUT clock 2026-10-20T02:51:14Z)

    >>> cat /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon1/temp1_max
    20000
    >>> cat /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon1/temp1_input
    27250               <- the reading is UNCHANGED and real: 27.25 C
    >>> cat /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon1/temp1_alarm
    1                   <- was 0 before the write

**The sensor did not move. The limit did.** `temp1_input` reads 27000 before and 27250 after --
that 0.25 C is the sensor's own quantisation on a room-temperature part, not heating.

The CLI, read at 15:51:35 NZDT (`work/r2-s6-alarm-asserted.log`):

    Environment Monitoring Status
    Overall Status: ***Fault***
    ...
    6   Temp: Front Right (Degrees C)            27        -11         20  FAULT
    7   Temp: Rear Right (Degrees C)             33        -11         70     Ok
    ... every other sensor Ok ...

and `show system` -> `Environment Status : ***Fault***`.

**The DUT's own log line, verbatim** (`show log tail 25`, same file):

    2026 Oct 20 02:51:17 daemon.crit awplus Platform: SENSOR Board Base - Temp: Front Right: Alarm asserted. Reading:27 Threshold:20 (Degrees C)

That is the same sentence, from the same platform layer, as the one the Test Engineer produced
on the x250 -- `Reading:27 Threshold:20`, naming the sensor. DUT 02:51:17 UTC + the measured
15 s skew = **box 15:51:32 NZDT**. The write was at box ~15:51:29, so the platform noticed
within about 3 seconds.

**Also read while asserted, over SNMP** (`work/r2-s7-snmp-while-asserted.log`, 15:52:28,
`tools/tb tool tb277 cksnmpmon snmpq get 10.38.22.34 ... --v2c --community testing123`), which
ties the CLI, the MIB and the trap to one and the same sensor index:

    .1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.3.1.4.1.100.6 = STRING: "Temp: Front Right"   description
    .1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.3.1.5.1.100.6 = INTEGER: 27                   reading
    .1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.3.1.6.1.100.6 = INTEGER: 20                   upper limit
    .1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.3.1.7.1.100.6 = INTEGER: 1                    status

Status **1** here against **2** in run 1's full walk of the same OID for all fourteen sensors
(`run1 work/s3-envmon-walk.log`): 2 is the healthy value, 1 is the fault value. The upper-limit
column follows the sysfs write, which is independent confirmation that the CLI, the MIB and
hwmon are three views of one value.

**STEP 4 PASS -- the over-temperature alarm asserted on a real, unmodified sensor reading.**

## STEP 5 -- THE TRAP, CAUGHT ON THE WIRE, AND ITS DE-ASSERT PARTNER

The whole of steps 4 and 6 happened inside ONE packet capture, so the assert and the clear are
two messages in one file taken by one instrument:

    tools/tb capture tb277 cksnmpmon eth3 300 r2-trap.pcap udp port 162
    box 15:51:01 -> 15:56:01 NZDT, "2 packets captured, 2 received by filter, 0 dropped"

Decoded with `tools/tb tool tb277 cksnmpmon snmpwire /tmp/cksnmpmon/r2-trap.pcap --port 162
--values --abs` (`work/r2-s11-trap-capture.log`). Verbatim:

    #1   15:51:31.396 10.38.22.34:44160 -> 10.38.22.33:162  SNMPv2c  community='testing123'  pdu=SNMPv2-Trap  msgData=PLAINTEXT
          1.3.6.1.2.1.1.3.0 = TimeTicks: 33293467
          1.3.6.1.6.3.1.1.4.1.0 = OID: 1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.0.5
          1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.3.1.1.1.100.6 = Gauge32: 1                    index
          1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.3.1.2.1.100.6 = Gauge32: 100                  resource 100 = AT-x960-56YSQ
          1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.3.1.3.1.100.6 = Gauge32: 6                    sensor 6
          1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.3.1.4.1.100.6 = STRING: Temp: Front Right     the sensor, by name
          1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.3.1.6.1.100.6 = INTEGER: 20                   the crossed upper limit
          1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.3.1.5.1.100.6 = INTEGER: 27                   the reading
    #2   15:53:01.597 10.38.22.34:44160 -> 10.38.22.33:162  SNMPv2c  community='testing123'  pdu=SNMPv2-Trap  msgData=PLAINTEXT
          1.3.6.1.2.1.1.3.0 = TimeTicks: 33302487
          1.3.6.1.6.3.1.1.4.1.0 = OID: 1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.0.6
          ... same five identity varbinds, same sensor ...
          1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.3.1.6.1.100.6 = INTEGER: 61                   the restored limit
          1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.3.1.5.1.100.6 = INTEGER: 27                   the same reading
    SUMMARY 2 SNMP message(s): 0 ENCRYPTED, 2 PLAINTEXT

**What this is, in plain words.** The switch sent an SNMPv2c notification from its own address
(10.38.22.34, the vlan10 interface) to the configured trap host, and the notification says which
sensor is in trouble, by name, with the temperature it read and the limit it crossed. That is
the trap this test case is named after. It was caught **on the wire**, not inferred from a log
line (STANDING-ORDERS §7).

**Two notification OIDs, and what each one means here.** Neither is one of the standard
notifications `snmpwire.py` annotates, so no name is claimed for them; what IS measured is which
one appears when:

| notification `snmpTrapOID.0` | observed when | upper-limit varbind |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.0.5` | the sensor went out of limits | 20 (the lowered limit) |
| `1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.0.6` | the sensor came back in limits | 61 (the restored limit) |

They sit directly under the AT-ENVMONv2 subtree `1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12` whose `.3.1`
temperature table run 1 walked in full, and they carry that table's own columns as varbinds.
🔥 **The fault and the clear are distinguishable ONLY by the value of `snmpTrapOID.0`** -- the
five identity varbinds are byte-identical and even the reading is the same 27 in both. A manager
(or a bench tool) that looks only at the varbind OIDs cannot tell "too hot" from "recovered".
This is the same trap-grading rule `tools/README.md` records for linkUp/linkDown and
risingAlarm/fallingAlarm, now confirmed for the environment traps too.

**Times line up on both clocks:**

| event | DUT log (UTC) | DUT + 15 s skew = box | trap on the wire (box) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| alarm asserted | 02:51:17 | 15:51:32 | **15:51:31.396** |
| alarm cleared | 02:52:47 | 15:53:02 | **15:53:01.597** |

**STEP 5 PASS -- the over-temperature trap was produced and captured.**

## STEP 6 -- restore the threshold; the alarm clears and the clear is also trapped

`work/r2-s8-restore-threshold.log`, box 15:52:50 NZDT:

    >>> cat /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon1/temp1_max
    20000
    >>> cat /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon1/temp1_alarm
    1
    >>> echo 61000 > /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon1/temp1_max      (DUT clock 2026-10-20T02:52:4xZ)
    >>> cat /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon1/temp1_max
    61000
    >>> od -c /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon1/temp1_max
    0000000    6   1   0   0   0  \n
    0000006
    >>> wc -c /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon1/temp1_max
    6 /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon1/temp1_max
    >>> cat /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon1/temp1_input
    27000

🔴 **RESTORE PROOF: `od -c` after the restore is byte-for-byte the `od -c` taken before the
change -- `6 1 0 0 0 \n`, six bytes.** Not "looks the same", the same bytes.

The de-assert arrived on the console while the shell was still open, and is quoted here exactly
as the device printed it (the line wrapped in the transcript; it is one log line):

    02:52:47 awplus Platform: SENSOR Board Base - Temp: Front Right: Alarm cleared. Reading:27 Threshold:61 (Degrees C)

and from `show log tail 15` at 15:53:2x (`work/r2-s9-after-restore.log`):

    2026 Oct 20 02:52:47 daemon.crit awplus Platform: SENSOR Board Base - Temp: Front Right: Alarm cleared. Reading:27 Threshold:61 (Degrees C)

The CLI is fully back:

    Overall Status: Normal
    6   Temp: Front Right (Degrees C)            27        -11         61     Ok
    show system -> Environment Status : Normal

and so is the MIB (`work/r2-s10-snmp-after-restore.log`, 15:53:4x): reading 27, **upper limit
back to 61**, **status back to 2**.

**A trap that asserts and never clears would itself be a finding. This one clears**, and the
clear is on the wire as trap #2 of step 5. **STEP 6 PASS.**

## STEP 7 -- side effects: did anything else on the unit react?

Watched deliberately, because a wrong limit could in principle drive a fan ramp or a thermal
protection action. Fan speeds, from `show system environment` at each stage:

| fan | before (15:46) | alarm asserted (15:51) | after restore (15:53) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| bank A Fan 1 / Fan 2 | 9342 / 9407 | 9424 / 9424 | 9358 / 9424 |
| bank B Fan 1 / Fan 2 | 10112 / 10037 | 10285 / 10285 | 10246 / 10207 |
| PSU 1 / PSU 2 | 4992 / 5920 | 4832 / 5568 | — |

**No ramp.** The chassis fans move by about 1-2% across all three samples, which is ordinary
regulation, and the PSU fans went DOWN while the alarm was asserted -- the opposite of a
thermal response. **No port was disabled, no module was powered off, nothing rebooted, and no
thermal-protection or shutdown message appears anywhere in `show log`.** The mechanism for that
is visible in the hwmon tree: the fan control points are separate attributes
(`temp1_auto_point1_temp = 90000`, `temp1_auto_point2_temp = 122000`) and the critical limit is
`temp1_crit = 100000`. **This case changed none of them** -- only `temp1_max`, the alarm
limit -- so the fan curve and the critical path were never involved. **STEP 7 PASS.**

## STEP 8 -- 🔴 FINDING: the traps are NOT gated by `snmp-server enable trap hw-monitoring`

This step started as the ordinary removal check that `satisfactory-level-of-testing` asks for --
take the feature away and prove it stops. It did not stop.

**8a. The group removes cleanly from running-config** (`work/r2-s12-group-removed.log`, 15:57:04):

    awplus(config)# no snmp-server enable trap hw-monitoring
    awplus# show running-config | include snmp
    snmp-server
    snmp-server community testing123
    snmp-server host 10.38.22.33 version 2c testing123

The `snmp-server enable trap hw-monitoring` line is gone; the community and the trap host are
untouched, so the transport is still configured and only the group is off. No `% ` line.

**8b. With the group removed, the same stimulus still produced both traps.**
Capture `tools/tb capture tb277 cksnmpmon eth3 150 r2-control.pcap udp port 162`, box
15:57:13 -> 15:59:43, **"2 packets captured"**. Stimulus `work/r2-s13-control-stimulus.log`:
`echo 20000` at DUT 02:57:17, `echo 61000` at DUT 02:57:49, the device logging
`Alarm asserted ... Threshold:20` at 02:57:18 and `Alarm cleared ... Threshold:61` at 02:58:18.
Decoded (`work/r2-s12-group-removed.log`):

    #1  15:57:32.341  ... OID: 1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.0.5  Temp: Front Right  limit 20  reading 27
    #2  15:58:32.499  ... OID: 1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.0.6  Temp: Front Right  limit 61  reading 27
    SUMMARY 2 SNMP message(s): 0 ENCRYPTED, 2 PLAINTEXT

**8c. The discriminator -- was the group ever required at all?**
"Removal did not take effect" and "the group was never what enabled these traps" predict the
same capture, so a third leg was run to tell them apart. The whole SNMP configuration was torn
down to the bare `snmp-server` line and then rebuilt with **only** the community and the host --
`snmp-server enable trap hw-monitoring` was never typed again
(`work/r2-s15-neverenabled.log`, 16:00:5x - 16:01:04):

    awplus(config)# no snmp-server host 10.38.22.33 version 2c testing123
    awplus(config)# no snmp-server community testing123
    awplus# show running-config | include snmp
    snmp-server                                   <- nothing else left
    awplus(config)# snmp-server community testing123 ro
    awplus(config)# snmp-server host 10.38.22.33 version 2c testing123
    awplus# show running-config | include snmp
    snmp-server
    snmp-server community testing123
    snmp-server host 10.38.22.33 version 2c testing123

Capture `r2-neverenabled.pcap`, box 16:01:12 -> 16:03:43, **"2 packets captured"**:

    #1   16:01:33.022 10.38.22.34:60899 -> 10.38.22.33:162  SNMPv2c  community='testing123'
          1.3.6.1.6.3.1.1.4.1.0 = OID: 1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.0.5
          ... = STRING: Temp: Front Right ... limit 20 ... reading 27
    #2   16:02:03.115 ... OID: 1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.0.6 ... limit 61 ... reading 27
    SUMMARY 2 SNMP message(s): 0 ENCRYPTED, 2 PLAINTEXT

**The finding, stated carefully.** On this build, **an AT-ENVMONv2 environment trap is sent to a
configured SNMPv2c trap host whether or not `snmp-server enable trap hw-monitoring` is in the
running configuration.** The command is accepted, is displayed, chains with other groups and
removes per-group (run 1 measured all of that and it is not disputed) -- but on the evidence of
two independent 150-second captures it **does not gate the traps it appears to gate**. An
operator who removes the group to stop these notifications will still receive them.

**What is NOT claimed, so nobody over-reads this:**
- Not that the group does nothing *at all*. Only the temperature sensor was stimulated. The
  `hw-monitoring` group may well gate fan, PSU or voltage notifications that this case never
  provoked.
- Not that any *other* trap group is unaffected. `rmon` (run 1) and the link traps (T44301) were
  measured separately and behaved as configured.
- One residual alternative remains open and is NOT excluded by this bench: that the group's
  state is only read at daemon or system start, so a removal cannot take effect until a reboot
  -- which would make the behaviour a latency rather than an absence of gating. Against that,
  five separate `snmp-server` configuration changes (each of which the device logs as
  `IMISH: [SCRIPT]snmp restart isis`) happened between the removal and the 8c capture, and the
  traps survived all of them. **Settling it needs a reload, which this campaign cannot do**
  (see "Reboot persistence"). It is written down here rather than resolved.
- The baseline of STEP 3 rules out the lazy explanation that these traps are periodic: with the
  group armed and the hardware healthy, 60 seconds produced zero packets. **Every trap in this
  case followed a threshold crossing within about 3 seconds.**

**STEP 8: the removal check FAILED as a product behaviour, and is reported as a finding.** It
is not graded as a failure of this case, whose subject is the temperature trap -- which works.

## STEP 9 -- repeatability

The assert/clear pair was produced **three times** from the same sensor at three different
configurations (group armed; group removed; group never re-added), and the device logged all
six transitions. From `show log tail 150` at 16:04:32 (`work/r2-s16-health-after.log`), the
complete set of `daemon.crit` lines for this case, verbatim:

    2026 Oct 20 02:51:17 daemon.crit awplus Platform: SENSOR Board Base - Temp: Front Right: Alarm asserted. Reading:27 Threshold:20 (Degrees C)
    2026 Oct 20 02:52:47 daemon.crit awplus Platform: SENSOR Board Base - Temp: Front Right: Alarm cleared. Reading:27 Threshold:61 (Degrees C)
    2026 Oct 20 02:57:18 daemon.crit awplus Platform: SENSOR Board Base - Temp: Front Right: Alarm asserted. Reading:27 Threshold:20 (Degrees C)
    2026 Oct 20 02:58:18 daemon.crit awplus Platform: SENSOR Board Base - Temp: Front Right: Alarm cleared. Reading:27 Threshold:61 (Degrees C)
    2026 Oct 20 03:01:19 daemon.crit awplus Platform: SENSOR Board Base - Temp: Front Right: Alarm asserted. Reading:27 Threshold:20 (Degrees C)
    2026 Oct 20 03:01:49 daemon.crit awplus Platform: SENSOR Board Base - Temp: Front Right: Alarm cleared. Reading:27 Threshold:61 (Degrees C)

**Every one of them reads `Reading:27`.** That is the whole case in one column: the temperature
never changed. Six transitions, six traps on the wire (2 + 2 + 2), no missed and no spurious
notification.

Two timing observations, recorded because they are the kind of thing a later run would
otherwise rediscover:
- **Assert latency is about 1-3 seconds** from the sysfs write (02:51:14 -> 02:51:17;
  02:57:17 -> 02:57:18; 03:01:17 -> 03:01:19).
- **De-assert latency varies, 1 s to 29 s** (02:52:46 -> 02:52:47, but 02:57:49 -> 02:58:18).
  The adt7476's `temp1_alarm` bit is sticky: it still read `1` immediately after the restoring
  write, and the platform cleared it on its own next poll. Nothing was wrong; a test that
  expected the clear within 5 seconds would simply be wrong about the device.

## STEP 10 -- persistence of the sysfs change: reasoned, NOT measured

Two separate questions, and they get different answers:

- **Does the lowered threshold survive a reload? No, and it cannot.** `/sys` is not stored
  anywhere: it is a kernel-synthesised filesystem rebuilt at every boot, and `temp1_max` is a
  live register of the adt7476 chip that the driver re-initialises from its platform data.
  There is nothing on flash to carry the value across. This is a statement of mechanism, and it
  is **NOT MEASURED here** -- see the next point for why.
- **Reboot persistence of the case's CLI configuration: NOT MEASURED**, exactly as run 1
  recorded. This case performed no reload and wrote nothing to startup-config. The campaign's
  whole L3 underlay (vlan10 on port1.0.10, vlan48 on port1.0.48) exists in running-config only
  -- boot config is `flash:/default.cfg` -- so a reload destroys the path to the trap receiver
  that the remaining cases need, and writing it to startup-config would change the standing
  bench (STANDING-ORDERS §4). Nothing new is asked here; the item is already on the queue.
- 🔴 **Consequence worth stating for the restore:** because the sysfs value is volatile, a
  reboot would also have "restored" it -- so a tester could be tempted to treat a reboot as the
  undo. **That is not a restore**, and it was not relied on. The value was written back by hand
  and verified byte for byte while the unit stayed up (STEP 6 and "Teardown" below).

